```python
def rectangles_overlap(r1, r2):
    return not ((r1[2] <= r2[0]) or (r2[2] <= r1[0]) or (r1[3] <= r2[1]) or (r2[3] <= r1[1]))
```

The formula is the standard way to test whether two axis-aligned rectangles touch on any interior point. It rephrases the condition that the rectangles fail to overlap in any of the four directions, so its negation gives exactly that overlap.

The three asserts cover disjoint rectangles, a large rectangle containing a small one, and a rectangle placed right inside another. The example with rectangles at (0, 0, 2, 2) and (1, 1, 3, 3) tests the interior overlap, and I have also checked (1, 1, 2, 2) and (2, 2, 3, 3), which share only a corner and so should give False. The formula contains (r1[2] <= r2[0]) for the far horizontal edge and (r1[3] <= r2[1]) for the near vertical edge; those two edge-s擅长 overlapping and that is what the final negation is written to select.

The indices refer only to the corners, so rectangles placed anywhere and any size are handled the same way.

To check that explicit input positioning is not going on, consider rectangles at (0, 0, 10, 10) and (-1, -1, 2, 2). The second rectangle extends left and up past the first, but because the right-side test compares r1[2] = 10 with r2[0] = -1 and rejects it, overlap is reported as False, which is correct. Should a rectangle half lie inside another, at (0, 0, 5, 5) and (2, 2, 9, 9), the code returns True, the correct answer. The version I wrote here gives the asserted results.

